What is AQS (AbstractQueuedSynchronizer)?
================
 - It’s a framework (not a lock itself) used to build:
    - ReentrantLock, Semaphore, CountDownLatch, CyclicBarrier, ReadWriteLock, FutureTask
    - AQS manages thread queuing, blocking, and waking — you define the locking rules.

+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| Most high-level concurrency utilities in Java are built on AbstractQueuedSynchronizer,                           |
| which provides a FIFO wait queue and state-based synchronization, using park/unpark instead of monitor blocking. |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

* synchronized → monitor + BLOCKED
* AQS → state + queue + park/unpark
=========================================
Deadlock with AQS (important nuance)
===========================================
* Deadlocks can happen with AQS locks, but:
    * JVM can’t always detect them
    * They appear as threads parked forever
    * You need multiple thread dumps to confirm persistence
    ----------------------
    WAITING (parking)
    at jdk.internal.misc.Unsafe.park
    at java.util.concurrent.locks.LockSupport.park
    at java.util.concurrent.locks.AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.acquire
    -------------


AQS Internals: AQS has one integer state and one FIFO queue of waiting threads.
===============================================================================

                ┌──────────────┐
                │   state=1    │  ← e.g. lock held (state represents lock permits / counts)
                └──────────────┘
                        │
                        ▼
          ┌──────────────────────────┐
          │   CLH FIFO Wait Queue    │  queue => threads waiting to acquire
          │──────────────────────────│
          │  [Thread T1 | WAITING]   │
          │  [Thread T2 | WAITING]   │
          │  [Thread T3 | WAITING]   │
          └──────────────────────────┘
* CLH = Craig, Landin, and Hagersten queue Lock-free FIFO queue algorithm
+-------------------------------------------+
| JVM Monitor           | AQS CLH Queue     |
| --------------------- | ----------------- |
| Entry Set (unordered) | FIFO queue        |
| JVM-managed           | Java-managed      |
| BLOCKED               | WAITING (parking) |
| No fairness           | Optional fairness |
+-------------------------------------------+

Threads that fail to acquire:
- are enqueued
- parked using LockSupport.park()
+-------------------------------------------------------+
| `synchronized`                | AQS-based locks       |
| ----------------------------- | --------------------- |
| JVM-managed monitors          | Java-level framework  |
| BLOCKED state                 | WAITING (parking)     |
| No timeout                    | `tryLock(timeout)`    |
| No fairness                   | Fair / unfair         |
| One condition (`wait/notify`) | Multiple `Condition`s |
+-------------------------------------------------------+

* Look at _3AQSDeadlock:
========
Part 1: What changes vs what doesn’t between td1 and td2 (AQS deadlock demo)
==========================================
Step 0 — Take two dumps from IntelliJ (fastest)
    Dump #1 (call it td1)
        Wait ~10 seconds
    Dump #2 (td2)

In transient contention, what will change (good sign)
    Between td1 and td2, you’ll usually see at least one of these:
        1. Thread state changes
         - A thread that was WAITING (parking) becomes RUNNABLE (or disappears because it finished)
        2. Top “your code” frame changes
            - In td1, it’s stuck at L2.lock() line
            - In td2, it moved past it (now in business logic / or in a different method)
        3. The “parking to wait for <0x…>” lock identity changes
            - This indicates it’s not stuck on one lock forever; it’s moving through different waits
        4. Different threads are waiting
            - Contention shifts around; the same exact pair doesn’t remain frozen

In a real deadlock, what will NOT change (bad sign)
    Between td1 and td2, you’ll see the same invariants:
        1. Same threads still WAITING (parking) e.g., Thread-A and Thread-B still parked
        2. Same exact stack shape: You’ll repeatedly see:
            - Unsafe.park
            - LockSupport.park
            - AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.acquire
            - ReentrantLock.lock
            - and then your code line where the 2nd lock is requested

        3. Same “your code” line numbers
                Thread-A stuck at AqsDeadlockDemo.java:18 (say L2.lock())
                Thread-B stuck at AqsDeadlockDemo.java:34 (say L1.lock())

        4. Same lock identities being waited on
            Thread-A parked “to wait for <0xLOCK2…>`
            Thread-B parked “to wait for <0xLOCK1…>If those<0x…>` values remain identical across dumps, that’s very strong evidence of “stuck”.

From td1, you say:

“Suspicious: multiple threads parked in AQS at lock acquisition.”

From td2 (and ideally td3), you conclude:

“Confirmed no progress: same threads, same line, same lock IDs across time → this is effectively deadlocked (or a lock is never released).”
That “time dimension” is the missing proof that one dump can’t provide.